  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 159 AM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nFriday-Saturday...The low pressure over Colorado will move \nnorthward into the Dakotas and Minnesota and produce a WAA wing \nacross eastern Iowa in association with a warm front. Scattered \nshowers and storms (40-80% coverage) are likely to form along \nthis boundary and move northeastward into Wisconsin. Overnight \nFriday, the mid-level trough finally reaches the northern edge \nof the ridge over the south-central CONUS and is ejected \neastward, pulling the low pressure into northern Iowa. With the \nenhanced surface convergence and a sufficiently moist air mass \n(PWATs near 2 inches), widespread storms (60-80% coverage) are \nexpected across the region through Saturday afternoon. Muggy \nconditions are favored to return with dew points in the upper \n60s to low 70s (60-90% chance).\n\nSunday-Tuesday...A surface pressure over Ontario pushes the low\npressure over Iowa southward into Missouri as the shortwave \nover the western CONUS finally propagates eastward. Mid-level \nCVA from the trough should support storm development north of \nthe low pressure Sunday into Monday before the system moves into\nIllinois and Indiana. CAA to kick in as the trough swings \nthrough the area, leading to a decrease in PoPs Tuesday and \nallowing cooler, drier air to move in.\n\nWith nearly daily PoPS through Monday and unseasonably high \nPWATs, additional rainfall of 2-3 inches is likely (50-80%) \nacross portions of eastern Iowa and northwest Illinois with \nlocalized areas of 5+ inches possible (5-15%). With most of the \nCWA already receiving 1-3+ inches over the last 48 hours, the \nflash flooding threat will become more elevated after each round\nof heavy rain, and small creeks and tributaries could see sharp\nrises in short periods of time.",
